11. Challenges Faced
Stopping bad data: the first version accepted anything typed in. Deciding which rules were worth adding without making the program annoying to use took some thought. I kept them to a non-empty name, a 10-digit phone and a basic email check.
Edit with blank fields: the edit function had to tell the difference between "keep the old value" and "the user typed something wrong". Blank now means keep, and anything typed is validated before the contact is changed.
Protecting saved data: if contacts.json was damaged and the program simply started with an empty book, the next save would overwrite the old data. Renaming the damaged file to .bak solved this.
Testing a menu program: it was slow to type the same inputs again and again, so I fed the inputs to the program from a script and checked the output.
Names as keys: using the name as the dictionary key is simple, but it means two people with the same name cannot be stored, and "ravi" and "Ravi" count as different. I decided to accept this limit to keep the design simple.
12. Learnings and Key Takeaways
Dictionaries are a good fit when data is looked up by a key.
Small helper functions for validation keep the main functions short and easy to read.
File handling needs error handling, because files can be missing or damaged.
Checking input before changing any data avoids half-updated records.
Writing test cases first, even a simple list, shows bugs and missing checks early.
A clear prompt and message for every step makes a console program much easier to use.
